I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the reusable analysis/refinement utilities first, then screen candidate phases and build a staged GSAS‑II refinement with audited checkpoints. I’ll keep unresolved components explicit rather than forcing a phase assignment.

The experiment is a Cu Kα reflection scan of a MnO₂/WO₃ mixture heated only to 300 °C, so I’ll explicitly test product tungstates alongside unreacted and polymorphic precursor phases. The first pass will use peak positions and systematic absences; intensity agreement will then decide among cell matches.

Peak-by-peak matching now supports two coherent families: monoclinic γ‑WO₃ explains the resolved 23–25° and 33–35° multiplets, while tetragonal pyrolusite MnO₂ explains independent peaks near 29.0°, 37.7°, 43.1°, 57.0°, and 59.7°. I’m moving these—and the closest polymorph/product alternatives—into matched whole-pattern GSAS‑II tests.

Whole-pattern tests reject the smaller-cell WO₃ polymorphs and confirm that both major families are required (removing WO₃ raises Rwp to ~38%; removing MnO₂ raises it to ~16%). MnWO₄ and Mn₃O₄ scales collapse to the nonpositive boundary. A triclinic WO₃ model currently fits materially better than the monoclinic model, so I’m carrying both forward through matched angular/profile tests before selecting the final symmetry.

The angular helper is now replaying each correction branch with the same cells, scales, and background. The refinement is numerically slow because the low-symmetry WO₃ cell has six metric variables, but it remains stable (no dropped variables or SVD singularities so far).

The width diagnostics favor two global terms: Gaussian W plus Lorentzian X. Each independently improves the entire pattern, both remain positive across 10–100°, and removing either causes a large degradation. Extra U/Y/Z terms are being challenged from that selected baseline; Z is already rejected because it is 99.9% correlated with X and refines negative.

Phase broadening tests support both finite size (~0.22 µm) and positive microstrain (~0.42%) for WO₃; each helps independently and their joint model remains stable. MnO₂ size runs to the upper boundary and its strain gives only a marginal, correlated improvement, so neither is retained. I’m now checking simple one-axis preferred orientation against the remaining intensity residuals.

I’m finishing the coordinate-stability tests now. The phase inventory is already narrowed to triclinic WO₃ plus pyrolusite MnO₂; these tests determine whether any atomic-coordinate refinement is genuinely supported or should be rolled back to the audited source structures.

The individual W-site refinements eventually converged, but each improves Rwp by only 0.01–0.03 percentage points and the combined-coordinate model oscillates. I’m therefore keeping the audited source coordinates; the data support unit cells and phase amounts much more strongly than subtle intracell shifts. Next I’m applying the same mature nuisance/profile model to the closest monoclinic WO₃ alternative so the symmetry choice is tested fairly.

The matched mature comparison converged cleanly: monoclinic WO₃ gives Rwp 7.60% versus 5.85% for triclinic WO₃, with the same angular/profile, crystallite-broadening, polarization, and MnO₂ texture terms. That is a large complete-pattern separation, so the triclinic assignment is secure at this resolution. I’m now doing the required mature-baseline rechecks for previously rejected minor/product phases.

The mature add-phase checks reject the proposed reaction products: Mn₃O₄ collapses to the scale boundary; Mn₃WO₆ worsens the fit with a tiny scale; MnWO₄ yields only a 0.0003-point Rwp change, far below a credible multi-peak benefit. The modeled crystalline inventory therefore remains WO₃ + MnO₂. I’m rerunning the angular branch selector on that final inventory, as required after the profile and texture changes.

The final angular recheck confirms the simpler zero-offset model: zero-only changes Rwp by just 0.0003 points, the displacement branch does not converge stably, and the joint model is 99.98% correlated. I’m retaining the mature baseline and now running matched leave-one-out tests on its optional profile, broadening, polarization, and texture terms before freezing the project.

The leave-one-out refinements are still running; they are deliberately expensive because each copy is allowed to reconverge the full remaining model. This guards against crediting a term merely because the other parameters were held at values optimized with it present.

Every retained optional term passed the matched removal test: removing W, X, polarization, WO₃ size, WO₃ strain, or MnO₂ [110] texture raises Rwp to 5.92–6.67% (baseline 5.85%). I’m now auditing the selected project before export, with particular attention to convergence, parameter signs, occupancies, and ADPs.

The selected project replayed cleanly (no dropped variables; maximum shift/esd 0.028), and both exported CIFs pass the composition, occupancy, symmetry, duplicate-site, and contact audits. The refined crystalline fractions are 72.405 wt% triclinic WO₃ and 27.595 wt% pyrolusite MnO₂; I’m writing the machine-readable result and the uncertainty-qualified report now.

Completed. The modeled crystalline composition is:

- Triclinic WO₃ (P-1): 72.405 ± 0.177 wt%
- Pyrolusite MnO₂ (P4₂/mnm): 27.595 ± 0.177 wt%

Rwp = 5.850%, Rp = 4.281%, GOF = 2.175. Fractions are normalized over crystalline phases; amorphous material and trace products cannot be quantified. No crystalline MnWO₄, Mn₃O₄, or Mn₃WO₆ was reliably supported.

